From a44012fd10b19ab362ee003a6763b95d5d22575b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OmarAfet <135471287+OmarAfet@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 21:40:10 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] feat: add `-Zpublic-api-hash` to skip compiling libraries
 whose dependencies did not change what they can see

With `-Zpublic-api-hash`, rustc computes a hash of everything in a library's metadata that its dependents can observe, and reports it in the artifact notification of the `.rmeta` file (rust-lang/rust#156940 and follow-ups). This makes Cargo use it: when a `lib`/`rlib` unit is dirty only because some of its dependencies were compiled again, and what it can see of each dependency did not change, compiling it again would produce the same outputs. Cargo then marks its outputs as up to date instead, and replays its cached messages like for a fresh unit.

What a unit sees of a dependency: the public API hash of a library; the output of a build script run (files the script writes are in the unit's dep-info); anything else, such as a proc macro, counts as changed whenever it was compiled again (output mtimes). This state is saved per unit in `public-api-hash-deps` (a JSON array), and the unit's own hash in `public-api-hash`, both in its fingerprint directory.

The public API hash leaves out span positions that dependents only use in diagnostics, so a unit is still compiled again when one of its cached diagnostics points into a file that is not its own source (from its dep-info) or a toolchain file.

To tell that only dependencies changed, the unit's own files are checked before its dependencies, but only with the flag: without it the order, and so the reported dirty reason, does not change.

`rustc` removes the message cache of a unit when its work is prepared, so the skip check is set up before that, keeps the cache in memory, and writes it back on a skip.

The new tests need a rustc that knows `-Zpublic-api-hash`, so they fail with today's nightly. They were checked by breaking the feature: never skipping fails the 3 skip tests, always skipping fails the 6 others, and dropping the diagnostics check fails only the warning test.
